1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,380
This little USB drive holds more than half

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:04,920
a billion dollars.

3
00:00:05,137 --> 00:00:06,997
And it has two guesses left.

4
00:00:07,412 --> 00:00:09,932
Get the password wrong two more times…

5
00:00:09,952 --> 00:00:11,912
and it destroys everything inside.

6
00:00:12,532 --> 00:00:13,032
Forever.

7
00:00:13,262 --> 00:00:13,802
Go on.

8
00:00:13,822 --> 00:00:14,742
Try.

9
00:00:15,184 --> 00:00:17,204
The man who owns it knows this.

10
00:00:17,364 --> 00:00:18,804
He's known it for years.

11
00:00:19,004 --> 00:00:20,784
And the drive is still sitting there…

12
00:00:20,804 --> 00:00:21,404
locked.

13
00:00:21,728 --> 00:00:23,008
So what's on it?

14
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:25,408
How did one forgotten password become one

15
00:00:25,428 --> 00:00:28,059
of the most expensive mistakes in tech?

16
00:00:28,079 --> 00:00:29,799
And when a team of hackers offered to

17
00:00:29,819 --> 00:00:31,299
break it open…

18
00:00:31,319 --> 00:00:32,679
why did he say no?

19
00:00:32,942 --> 00:00:36,182
This is the story of the drive that never forgets.

20
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:39,850
Meet Stefan Thomas.

21
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:41,150
A programmer.

22
00:00:41,750 --> 00:00:45,190
Back in 2011, almost nobody had heard of Bitcoin.

23
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:47,530
And Stefan made an animated video

24
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:49,410
explaining what it was.

25
00:00:49,509 --> 00:00:49,989
Yes.

26
00:00:50,009 --> 00:00:50,709
A cartoon.

27
00:00:50,729 --> 00:00:51,829
Like this one.

28
00:00:52,362 --> 00:00:54,462
And for that video, he was paid seven

29
00:00:54,482 --> 00:00:56,142
thousand and two bitcoin.

30
00:00:56,527 --> 00:00:59,307
Back then, bitcoin was worth almost nothing.

31
00:00:59,407 --> 00:01:01,107
So seven thousand of them?

32
00:01:01,127 --> 00:01:02,127
Pocket money.

33
00:01:02,441 --> 00:01:04,041
But Stefan was careful.

34
00:01:04,341 --> 00:01:06,041
He put the keys to those coins on a

35
00:01:06,061 --> 00:01:08,621
special drive called an IronKey.

36
00:01:08,649 --> 00:01:12,069
And the IronKey is not a normal USB stick.

37
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:14,189
It's built like a tiny safe.

38
00:01:14,449 --> 00:01:16,129
Military-grade encryption.

39
00:01:16,549 --> 00:01:18,009
A secure chip inside.

40
00:01:18,469 --> 00:01:20,469
And one very famous rule.

41
00:01:20,845 --> 00:01:21,845
Ten guesses.

42
00:01:22,265 --> 00:01:24,545
Get the password wrong ten times in a row,

43
00:01:24,565 --> 00:01:27,125
and the drive wipes its own secret key.

44
00:01:27,425 --> 00:01:28,745
The data is gone.

45
00:01:28,965 --> 00:01:29,845
No undo.

46
00:01:30,165 --> 00:01:31,045
No reset.

47
00:01:31,245 --> 00:01:32,685
No customer service.

48
00:01:33,121 --> 00:01:35,521
So Stefan picked a strong password.

49
00:01:35,781 --> 00:01:37,961
He wrote it down on a piece of paper.

50
00:01:38,298 --> 00:01:38,598
And then…

51
00:01:38,618 --> 00:01:40,098
he lost the paper.

52
00:01:41,247 --> 00:01:43,167
At first, it didn't matter much.

53
00:01:43,267 --> 00:01:44,507
Bitcoin was cheap.

54
00:01:44,585 --> 00:01:46,505
But then bitcoin started climbing.

55
00:01:46,845 --> 00:01:48,025
A hundred dollars.

56
00:01:48,065 --> 00:01:48,885
A thousand.

57
00:01:48,905 --> 00:01:49,965
Ten thousand.

58
00:01:50,049 --> 00:01:52,089
And every time the price went up,

59
00:01:52,109 --> 00:01:54,469
that little drive got more valuable…

60
00:01:54,489 --> 00:01:55,049
and more locked.

61
00:01:55,069 --> 00:01:57,671
So Stefan started guessing.

62
00:01:57,871 --> 00:01:59,291
His usual passwords.

63
00:01:59,451 --> 00:02:01,031
The ones he always used.

64
00:02:01,228 --> 00:02:01,908
Guess one.

65
00:02:01,928 --> 00:02:02,348
Wrong.

66
00:02:02,368 --> 00:02:03,088
Guess two.

67
00:02:03,108 --> 00:02:03,408
Wrong.

68
00:02:03,428 --> 00:02:03,728
Three…

69
00:02:03,748 --> 00:02:04,048
four…

70
00:02:04,048 --> 00:02:04,508
five…

71
00:02:04,528 --> 00:02:05,068
six…

72
00:02:05,088 --> 00:02:05,388
seven…

73
00:02:05,408 --> 00:02:06,388
Eight guesses.

74
00:02:06,408 --> 00:02:07,308
Eight misses.

75
00:02:08,206 --> 00:02:09,186
Two left.

76
00:02:10,047 --> 00:02:16,867
He told the New York Times: “I would just

77
00:02:16,887 --> 00:02:20,673
lay in bed and think about it.

78
00:02:20,693 --> 00:02:20,993
Then I would go to the computer with some

79
00:02:20,993 --> 00:02:21,293
new strategy, and it wouldn't work,

80
00:02:21,293 --> 00:02:21,593
and I would be desperate again.”

81
00:02:21,593 --> 00:02:22,763
By 2021,

82
00:02:22,963 --> 00:02:25,043
the coins on that drive were worth about

83
00:02:25,063 --> 00:02:27,703
two hundred and twenty million dollars.

84
00:02:27,943 --> 00:02:31,183
And at bitcoin's peak, in October 2025?

85
00:02:31,683 --> 00:02:33,623
Close to nine hundred million.

86
00:02:34,213 --> 00:02:35,633
So why not just…

87
00:02:35,653 --> 00:02:36,573
break it open?

88
00:02:36,933 --> 00:02:39,713
Plug it into a computer and try a million passwords?

89
00:02:40,214 --> 00:02:42,134
Because the IronKey remembers.

90
00:02:42,548 --> 00:02:44,788
Every wrong guess is counted inside its

91
00:02:44,808 --> 00:02:45,948
secure chip.

92
00:02:46,068 --> 00:02:49,008
Unplug it, wait a year, plug it back in…

93
00:02:49,028 --> 00:02:50,848
the counter is still there.

94
00:02:51,171 --> 00:02:53,671
The chip is sealed under tough epoxy,

95
00:02:53,911 --> 00:02:56,542
built to resist exactly this kind of attack.

96
00:02:56,562 --> 00:02:58,382
Even Kingston, the company that makes

97
00:02:58,402 --> 00:03:00,962
IronKeys today, said it couldn't help.

98
00:03:01,202 --> 00:03:02,042
No backdoor.

99
00:03:02,242 --> 00:03:03,602
No master password.

100
00:03:04,180 --> 00:03:05,380
So it's impossible.

101
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:06,120
Right?

102
00:03:06,617 --> 00:03:08,717
Want to know if anyone ever cracks me?

103
00:03:08,737 --> 00:03:09,617
Subscribe.

104
00:03:09,677 --> 00:03:10,357
I'll wait.

105
00:03:10,377 --> 00:03:11,937
I'm very good at waiting.

106
00:03:12,292 --> 00:03:13,352
Not quite.

107
00:03:13,672 --> 00:03:14,892
Because someone has pulled off something

108
00:03:14,912 --> 00:03:16,252
like this before.

109
00:03:16,438 --> 00:03:19,238
Meet Joe Grand, a hardware hacker.

110
00:03:19,538 --> 00:03:20,618
In 2021,

111
00:03:20,738 --> 00:03:22,538
he took on a challenge from a man named

112
00:03:22,558 --> 00:03:23,607
Dan Reich.

113
00:03:23,627 --> 00:03:25,907
Dan's crypto wallet, a little device

114
00:03:25,927 --> 00:03:28,467
called a Trezor, held about two million

115
00:03:28,487 --> 00:03:30,027
dollars in crypto.

116
00:03:30,467 --> 00:03:32,027
And he'd forgotten the PIN.

117
00:03:32,139 --> 00:03:33,899
Dan and a friend had already guessed wrong

118
00:03:33,919 --> 00:03:35,199
twelve times.

119
00:03:35,799 --> 00:03:36,679
After sixteen,

120
00:03:36,899 --> 00:03:38,699
the wallet would wipe itself.

121
00:03:38,788 --> 00:03:40,548
And every wrong guess made them wait

122
00:03:40,568 --> 00:03:42,688
longer before the next try.

123
00:03:42,856 --> 00:03:44,116
So Joe didn't guess.

124
00:03:44,216 --> 00:03:46,016
He attacked the hardware itself.

125
00:03:46,233 --> 00:03:48,673
He found that during a firmware update,

126
00:03:48,733 --> 00:03:50,953
the wallet copies its PIN into the chip's

127
00:03:50,973 --> 00:03:52,253
temporary memory.

128
00:03:52,633 --> 00:03:54,653
That memory is protected…

129
00:03:54,673 --> 00:03:56,833
but Joe hit the chip with a tiny,

130
00:03:56,853 --> 00:03:59,493
perfectly timed glitch in its power,

131
00:03:59,573 --> 00:04:01,711
and tricked it into letting him read it.

132
00:04:01,731 --> 00:04:04,431
It took about twelve weeks of trial and error.

133
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:05,180
And then…

134
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:07,040
the PIN popped up on his screen.

135
00:04:07,334 --> 00:04:08,534
Two million dollars.

136
00:04:08,554 --> 00:04:09,194
Back.

137
00:04:09,528 --> 00:04:10,808
But here's the catch.

138
00:04:11,208 --> 00:04:13,148
That trick used a flaw Trezor had already

139
00:04:13,168 --> 00:04:15,288
fixed back in 2017.

140
00:04:15,748 --> 00:04:17,668
Stefan's IronKey is a different,

141
00:04:17,688 --> 00:04:19,248
tougher machine.

142
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:23,220
Then, in 2023, a security company called

143
00:04:23,240 --> 00:04:25,372
Unciphered said it had done it.

144
00:04:25,392 --> 00:04:26,712
Working with Wired,

145
00:04:26,892 --> 00:04:29,292
they took an IronKey of the same model as

146
00:04:29,312 --> 00:04:32,192
Stefan's, carved out its secure chip,

147
00:04:32,212 --> 00:04:35,018
dissolved the protective epoxy with acid…

148
00:04:35,038 --> 00:04:36,698
…and ran about two hundred trillion

149
00:04:36,718 --> 00:04:38,198
password guesses.

150
00:04:39,189 --> 00:04:40,209
It opened.

151
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:42,980
So they made Stefan an offer: let us

152
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,580
unlock your drive.

153
00:04:44,686 --> 00:04:45,026
And Stefan…

154
00:04:45,046 --> 00:04:46,246
said no.

155
00:04:47,149 --> 00:04:50,409
Why would anyone say no to half a billion dollars?

156
00:04:50,656 --> 00:04:52,556
Because, according to reports,

157
00:04:52,676 --> 00:04:54,856
he had already hired another team.

158
00:04:55,076 --> 00:04:56,856
And he'd agreed to give them a share if

159
00:04:56,876 --> 00:04:57,956
they succeeded.

160
00:04:58,178 --> 00:05:00,338
So somewhere, maybe right now,

161
00:05:00,358 --> 00:05:02,858
someone may be trying to break into that drive.

162
00:05:03,161 --> 00:05:04,761
Has anyone succeeded?

163
00:05:05,081 --> 00:05:06,881
As of today, there's no public

164
00:05:06,901 --> 00:05:09,201
confirmation that it's been opened.

165
00:05:09,785 --> 00:05:11,842
And Stefan isn't the only one.

166
00:05:11,862 --> 00:05:14,542
In Wales, a man named James Howells says

167
00:05:14,562 --> 00:05:16,862
he threw away a hard drive with around

168
00:05:16,882 --> 00:05:19,162
eight thousand bitcoin on it,

169
00:05:19,182 --> 00:05:20,602
back in 2013.

170
00:05:20,962 --> 00:05:22,762
It's buried in a landfill.

171
00:05:23,062 --> 00:05:25,762
He spent years in court trying to dig it up.

172
00:05:25,782 --> 00:05:27,922
In 2025, he lost.

173
00:05:28,339 --> 00:05:30,259
One estimate, from the blockchain firm

174
00:05:30,279 --> 00:05:33,039
Chainalysis, suggested that up to one in

175
00:05:33,059 --> 00:05:35,579
five bitcoins may be lost forever.

176
00:05:36,139 --> 00:05:37,199
Forgotten passwords.

177
00:05:37,679 --> 00:05:38,899
Thrown-out drives.

178
00:05:39,619 --> 00:05:41,499
Owners who never told anyone.

179
00:05:41,709 --> 00:05:43,869
So how do you make sure this never happens

180
00:05:43,889 --> 00:05:44,717
to you?

181
00:05:44,737 --> 00:05:45,237
One.

182
00:05:45,257 --> 00:05:46,837
Use a password manager,

183
00:05:47,077 --> 00:05:49,277
so your brain isn't the only backup.

184
00:05:49,337 --> 00:05:49,977
Two.

185
00:05:49,997 --> 00:05:51,737
Write down your recovery codes,

186
00:05:51,757 --> 00:05:54,157
and keep copies in two safe places.

187
00:05:54,537 --> 00:05:55,974
Not one piece of paper.

188
00:05:55,994 --> 00:05:56,614
Three.

189
00:05:56,634 --> 00:05:58,447
Test your backup before you need it.

190
00:05:58,467 --> 00:05:59,367
And four.

191
00:05:59,567 --> 00:06:01,467
Make sure someone you trust knows how to

192
00:06:01,487 --> 00:06:04,467
find it, if something ever happens to you.

193
00:06:04,604 --> 00:06:06,944
Stefan called it a painful memory,

194
00:06:06,964 --> 00:06:08,964
and said he hopes others can learn from

195
00:06:08,984 --> 00:06:10,004
his mistakes.

196
00:06:10,404 --> 00:06:13,224
He said he was depressed for a few weeks…

197
00:06:13,244 --> 00:06:14,804
then decided to look forward,

198
00:06:14,824 --> 00:06:16,104
and keep building.

199
00:06:16,583 --> 00:06:18,143
So here's my question.

200
00:06:18,323 --> 00:06:19,103
Two guesses.

201
00:06:19,423 --> 00:06:21,403
More than half a billion dollars.

202
00:06:21,523 --> 00:06:22,943
Would you try them yourself…

203
00:06:22,963 --> 00:06:24,963
or hand the drive to a hacker?

204
00:06:25,123 --> 00:06:26,423
Tell me in the comments.

205
00:06:26,644 --> 00:06:28,824
And subscribe, because next time,

206
00:06:28,844 --> 00:06:30,444
we're going to that landfill.

207
00:06:30,846 --> 00:06:32,716
And I thought my sentence was bad.

208
00:06:32,824 --> 00:06:33,824
Two guesses.

209
00:06:33,844 --> 00:06:34,964
I'll be waiting.
